**Endpoint anatomy**
- `@Endpoint(id = "features")` registers an endpoint at `/actuator/features`
- `@ReadOperation` — handles GET requests; return value serialized to JSON
- `@WriteOperation` — handles POST requests; method parameters become request body fields
- `@DeleteOperation` — handles DELETE requests; for state removal
- `@Selector` — captures path variables: `/actuator/features/{name}`

**Implementation example**

```java
@Component
@Endpoint(id = "features")
public class FeatureFlagsEndpoint {

    private final FeatureFlagService flags;

    public FeatureFlagsEndpoint(FeatureFlagService flags) {
        this.flags = flags;
    }

    @ReadOperation
    public Map<String, Boolean> all() {
        return flags.snapshot();
    }

    @ReadOperation
    public Boolean one(@Selector String name) {
        return flags.isEnabled(name);
    }

    @WriteOperation
    public void toggle(@Selector String name, boolean enabled) {
        flags.set(name, enabled);
    }
}
```

**Registration and exposure**
- Mark with `@Component` so Spring discovers it
- Add `features` to `management.endpoints.web.exposure.include`
- Apply the same Spring Security rules as other Actuator endpoints
- For JMX-only or web-only restriction, use `@WebEndpoint` or `@JmxEndpoint`

**Inject MeterRegistry**
- Spring Boot auto-configures the registry based on classpath
- Use constructor injection like any other bean
- Common metric types: Counter, Gauge, Timer, DistributionSummary

**Counter — monotonically increasing event count**

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final Counter ordersPlaced;
    private final Counter ordersFailed;

    public OrderService(MeterRegistry registry) {
        this.ordersPlaced = Counter.builder("orders.placed")
            .description("Total orders successfully placed")
            .tag("region", System.getenv("REGION"))
            .register(registry);
        this.ordersFailed = registry.counter("orders.failed");
    }

    public void placeOrder(Order order) {
        try {
            persist(order);
            ordersPlaced.increment();
        } catch (Exception e) {
            ordersFailed.increment();
            throw e;
        }
    }
}
```

**Timer — latency histograms with percentile estimates**

```java
Timer dbTimer = Timer.builder("orders.db.save")
    .publishPercentiles(0.5, 0.95, 0.99)
    .publishPercentileHistogram()
    .register(registry);

dbTimer.record(() -> repository.save(order));
```

**Declarative timing with @Timed**
- Annotate any Spring-managed method: `@Timed(value = "orders.process", percentiles = {0.95, 0.99})`
- Requires `TimedAspect` bean: `@Bean public TimedAspect timedAspect(MeterRegistry r) { return new TimedAspect(r); }`
- Skips boilerplate for common timer instrumentation
